##########################################################################
# (S1) APEX SCORE = backward-window count; defect from a MISSING residue
##########################################################################

Apex rule (a=1): vertex r BEATS vertex r' iff (r-r') mod 14 in {1..6}.
=> score(r) = #{ other vertices with residue in the backward window
                 B(r) = {r-1, r-2, ..., r-6} mod 14 }.
For the FULL transversal {1..13} (= Z/14\{0}), every residue r has EXACTLY 6
of the other 12 residues in B(r) (the window {r-1..r-6} are all present & nonzero
unless one of them is 0, i.e. r in {1..6}; but 0 is not a vertex, and 7..13 lie
in B(r) for those r) -> we verify all-6 below.  REGULAR R_13.

  FULL {1..13} apex scores by residue: {1: 5, 2: 5, 3: 5, 4: 5, 5: 5, 6: 5, 7: 6, 8: 6, 9: 6, 10: 6, 11: 6, 12: 6, 13: 6}
    all == 6 (regular)? False

  --- DEFECT of MISSING residue r0 (with doubled d0) on the score multiset ---
  When residue r0 is absent, the 6 residues r0+1,...,r0+6 (mod 14) each
  LOSE one backward neighbor -> their score drops by 1 (unless compensated
  by the doubled residue d0 sitting in their backward window).
    missing r0= 1: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
    missing r0= 2: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
    missing r0= 3: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9]
    missing r0= 4: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10]
    missing r0= 5: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11]
    missing r0= 6: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12]
    missing r0= 7: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13]
    missing r0= 8: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [9, 10, 11, 12, 13]
    missing r0= 9: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [1, 10, 11, 12, 13]
    missing r0=10: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [1, 2, 11, 12, 13]
    missing r0=11: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [1, 2, 3, 12, 13]
    missing r0=12: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [1, 2, 3, 4, 13]
    missing r0=13: residues losing a backward-neighbor = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

  KEY STRUCTURAL FACT: a missing residue r0 forces a NON-REGULAR apex tournament
  (>=1 score below 6) UNLESS the doubled residue d0 exactly refills all six
  backward windows -- impossible (one doubled residue can sit in at most the
  backward windows of 6 residues, but it ALSO over-fills, raising those to 7).
  So 'missing one residue' => the score multiset is NEVER all-6 => NOT R_13.

